To confirm that the \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(m\) -dobdc) frameworks are indeed structurally analogous to \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) , powder \(\mathbf{X}\) -ray and neutron diffraction experiments were used to solve the crystal structure of \(\mathbf{Co}_{2}(m\) -dobdc) (Figure 2). Similar to \(\mathbf{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) , \(\mathbf{Co}_{2}(m\) -dobdc) possesses helical chains of \(C0^{2+}\) centers running parallel to the crystallographic \(c\) axis. Upon activation and removal of a bound DMF molecule, each metal center is ligated by a combination of oxido and carboxylate donors in a square pyramidal geometry, with open coordination sites directed into the
one-dimensional hexagonal channels of the framework. Based on the change in point group symmetry of the linker from \(C_{2h}\) in \(\mathrm{H}_{4}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) to \(C_{2\nu}\) in \(\mathrm{H}_{4}(m\) -dobdc), a change in the space group from \(R\overline{{3}}\overline{{3}}\) in \(\mathbf{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) to \(R_{3}m\) in \(\mathbf{Co}_{2}(m\) -dobdc)  is  observed.44  Additional  structural differences between the two compounds are apparent.9e For instance, the orientation of the carboxylate groups of the linker is changed, with the \({\mathrm{CO}}_{2}^{\,\,\,-}\) unit twisting out of the plane of the aromatic ring by approximately \(12.5^{\circ}\) in \(\mathbf{Co}_{2}(m\) -dobdc), as compared to just \(3.5^{\circ}\) in \(\mathbf{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) . Furthermore, metal centers in \(\mathbf{Co}_{2}(m\) -dobdc) that face into  the  same  pore  align  directly  along  the crystallographic \(b\) axis, resulting in a \(\mathbf{Co}{\cdots}\mathbf{Co}\) separation of 14 o(1) Å across the channels. In \(\mathbf{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) , the metals are offset from each other by one third of a twist in the chain, leading to a \(\mathbf{Co}{\cdots}\mathbf{Co}\) separation of 15.24(8) Å across the channels. A structureless Le Bail refinement of the other \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(m\) -dobdc) \(\mathbf{\Psi}(\mathbf{M}\ =\ \mathbf{M}\mathbf{g},\) , Mn, Fe, Ni) compounds afforded related unit cells, confirming that the entire series is isostructural.
With such similar crystal structures, the surface areas would be expected to be similar for the two types of frameworks. Indeed, a geometric approximation of the surface areas for \(\mathbf{Co}_{2}(m\) -dobdc) and \(\mathbf{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) predicts them to both be 1297 m2/g.23 Low-pressure \(\Nu_{2}\) adsorption isotherms  collected  at \(77\ \mathrm{~K~}\) reveal  type  I  isotherms characteristic of microporous solids for the \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(m\) -dobdc) \(\mathbf{\Psi}(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{\Psi}=\mathbf{\Psi}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}\) , Fe, Co, Ni) compounds (Figure S1). Langmuir and BET surface areas were calculated (Table 1) and in each case the results are comparable to those reported for \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) .4b,4c,9e,24  Based  on  the  similar  framework structure, this indicates full evacuation of the pores and complete desolvation of the framework. Furthermore, the \(\mathbf{Co}_{2}(m\) -dobdc) BET surface area of \(1264~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\) is very close to the predicted surface area of \(1297~\mathrm{\m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\) from the geometric calculations. Achieving complete activation of the \(\mathrm{Mg}\) analogue has proven challenging and all efforts have thus far resulted in materials with lower than expected surface areas. As a result, the remaining data will  focus  only  on  the  other  four \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(m\) -dobdc) compounds.
\(\mathbf{H}_{2}\) Adsorption Isotherms. To probe the potentially modified  electronic  structures  at  the  open  metal coordination sites, low-pressure \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) adsorption isotherms were measured for the four \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(m\) -dobdc) \(\mathbf{\Psi}(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{\Psi}=\mathbf{\Psi}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}\) , Fe, Co, Ni) frameworks at 77 K and 87 K (Figures S2o–S23). As for the \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) series,4a \(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(m\) -dobdc) has the greatest
\(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) uptake at \(77\,\mathrm{~K~}\) and 1 bar, followed by Fe, Mn, and Co compounds, respectively. Although the \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) frameworks have a higher \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) uptake at 1 bar, in the very low-pressure regime, \(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(m\mathrm{-dobdc})\) has a significantly higher uptake than \(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) at both 77 and \(87\,\mathrm{~K~}\) (Figure S27). This indicates that the interaction of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) with the exposed \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) cations ion the surface of \(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(m\mathrm{-dobdc})\) is stronger than in \(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\); since these frameworks have about the same surface area, another effect must be influencing the binding strength of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\).
Isosteric heats of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) adsorption were calculated in order to gain insight into this increased low-pressure \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) adsorption and confirm whether there is indeed a stronger interaction between \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and the metal centers in the \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(m\mathrm{-dobdc})\) frameworks. In order to determine the isosteric heat of adsorption \(\left(Q_{\mathrm{st}}\right)\) using the Clausius-Clapeyron relation, \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) isotherm data at 77 and \(87\,\mathrm{~K~}\) were fit with either a dual-site Langmuir-Freundlich equation or a tri-site Langmuir equation. Similar to \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\), the isosteric heat of adsorption plots for \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(m\mathrm{-dobdc})\) imply a nearly constant \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) binding enthalpy until a loading of \(0.7{-}0.8\;\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{M}^{2+}\), followed by a sharp decrease as all exposed metal cations become occupied and only weaker adsorption sites are available (Figure 3). The inflection points range from \({\sim}0.75\;\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{M}\) in \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(m\mathrm{-dobdc})\) to \({\sim}0.85\;\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{M}\) in \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(m\mathrm{-dobdc})\), which corresponds with the fraction of metal sites available for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) binding. These
