Increasing the binding enthalpy of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) in metal-organic frameworks is important for practical applications, as a binding enthalpy of \(-15\) to \(-20\ \mathrm{kJ/mol}\) is predicted to be optimal for the on-board storage of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) at ambient temperatures. The low-coverage isosteric heats of adsorption of \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}(m\mathrm{-dobdc})\), \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(m\mathrm{-dobdc})\), \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(m\mathrm{-dobdc})\), and \(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(m\mathrm{-dobdc})\) are \(-10.3\), \(-11.1\), \(-11.6\), and \(-12.3\ \mathrm{kJ/mol}\), respectively. This trend in binding enthalpies for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) is consistent with the Irving-Williams series, which predicts that high-spin complexes increase in stability moving from Group 7 to 10. The trend further mirrors that observed for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) binding within \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) frameworks (Table 2). Calculated isosteric heat of adsorption values are highly sensitive to the fitting equation used, the temperatures at which data is collected and the quality of the fits. Given this, the values for the \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) series were calculated from isotherms collected in this work (except \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\), which was previously reported by our group and fit using the same method) in order to maintain consistency in the collected isotherms and the manner in which they are fit. These values all agree with the literature values for the \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) series except for \(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\), which we found to be \(1\ \mathrm{kJ/mol}\) less than the literature value of \(-12.9\ \mathrm{kJ/mol}\). This discrepancy most likely arises from a difference in data collection, isotherm temperatures, and equation used to fit the data. Consequently, our data that was collected and fit in the same manner as the \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(m\mathrm{-dobdc})\) data presented here is used for comparison. Importantly, the isosteric heats of adsorption for the \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(m\mathrm{-dobdc})\) compounds are, on average, \({\sim}1.0\ \mathrm{kJ/mol}\) stronger than in the corresponding \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) frameworks (Table 2) and are as much as \(1.5\ \mathrm{kJ/mol}\) stronger in the case of Mn, which also has the largest percent increase \((17\%)\) in binding enthalpy.
