Increasing the \( \mathrm{D}_{2} \) loading in \( \mathrm{Co}_{2}(m \)-dobdc) revealed several secondary binding sites. At a loading of 1.25 \( \mathrm{D}_{2} \) per metal, a second binding site (site II) adjacent to the primary site becomes populated (Figure 4b). This can be attributed to a \( \mathrm{D}_{2} \cdots \mathrm{D}_{2} \) interaction based on a short \( \mathrm{D}_{2} \cdots \mathrm{D}_{2} \) separation of \( 2.88(4) \, \mathring{\mathrm{A}} \), combined with a \( \mathrm{D}_{2} \cdots \mathrm{O} \) interaction occurring at a separation of \( 3.28(6) \, \mathring{\mathrm{A}} \) from the nearest framework oxygen atom. Site II is in a similar location to the secondary binding sites observed in \( \mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc}) \) and \( \mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc}) \),6c whereby the second bound \( \mathrm{D}_{2} \) appears to rely on \( \mathrm{D}_{2} \cdots \mathrm{D}_{2} \) interactions in all cases. The site I to site II \( \mathrm{D}_{2} \cdots \mathrm{D}_{2} \) separation, however, of \( 3.05(2) \, \mathring{\mathrm{A}} \) in \( \mathrm{Co}_{2}(m \)-dobdc) at a loading of 2.25 \( \mathrm{D}_{2} \) per metal is significantly shorter than the \( 3.16(2) \, \mathring{\mathrm{A}} \) observed at the same loading in \( \mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc}) \) or the \( 3.16(8) \, \mathring{\mathrm{A}} \) arising for a similar loading of 1.2 \( \mathrm{D}_{2} \) per metal in \( \mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc}) \). This closer site I to site II separation in \( \mathrm{Co}_{2}(m \)-dobdc) is most likely a polarization effect, as the more strongly bound \( \mathrm{D}_{2} \) residing at site I has a larger induced dipole, leading to a stronger interaction with a \( \mathrm{D}_{2} \) molecule adsorbed at site II.
A third binding site (site III) located approximately \( 3.08 \, \mathring{\mathrm{A}} \) above the mean plane of the aromatic ring of the \( m \)-dobdc4- linker becomes populated at higher loadings of \( \mathrm{D}_{2} \). This site is equidistant (3.25(6) Å) to two \( \mathrm{D}_{2} \) molecules at neighboring site I positions in \( \mathrm{Co}_{2}(m \)-dobdc), whereas in \( \mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc}) \), the different symmetry leads to two
Neutron diffraction experiments intended to elucidate binding sites are generally performed using \( \mathrm{D}_{2} \) instead of \( \mathrm{H}_{2} \) because the large incoherent scattering cross-section of \( \mathrm{H} \) atoms contributes strongly to the background and reduces data quality. In addition, the coherent scattering cross-section of a \( \mathrm{D} \) atom is approximately four times larger than that of an \( \mathrm{H} \) atom. While it has been shown through gas adsorption measurements that metal-organic frameworks tend to adsorb \( \mathrm{D}_{2} \) slightly more strongly than \( \mathrm{H}_{2} \),28 it has been assumed that both molecules behave similarly when binding, only displaying extremely minor differences in interaction distances. In order to test this assumption, we measured and successfully refined the
structure of \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(m\mathrm{-dobdc})\) dosed with a low loading of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\). We believe this is the first structural data from powder neutron diffraction reported for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) in a metal-organic framework. As expected from the \(\mathrm{D}_{2}\) data, a dose below 1.0 \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) per metal site resulted in binding exclusively at site I. Interestingly, the center of the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) density was found to be 2.26(4) Å from the Co atom, perhaps slightly longer than the \(\mathrm{Co} \cdots \mathrm{D}_{2}\) distance of 2.23(5) Å, although still within the associated error. Whether this is indicative of a slightly stronger interaction with \(\mathrm{D}_{2}\) is somewhat unclear due to the resolution of the data; however, it does confirm that \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{D}_{2}\) can be expected to behave similarly for the purposes of structural determination.
Inelastic  Neutron  Scattering.  Inelastic  neutron scattering (INS) experiments were carried out to probe the site-specific binding properties of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) . Data for various loadings of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) after subtraction of the spectra for the evacuated materials are shown in Figure 5. At loadings up to and including 1.0 \(n\mathrm{-}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) (corresponding to a 3:1 ortho\(\mathrm{H}_{2}{\cdot}p a r a{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) ratio) molecule per metal atom, two lowenergy rotational lines are apparent at 7.8(1) and 9.5(1) meV for \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(m\mathrm{-dobdc})\) and 7.5(1) and 9.3(1) meV for \(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(m\mathrm{-dobdc})\) .  These  features  are  similar  to  those observed in INS spectra for several compounds in the \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) series at low loadings, and have been assigned to transitions occurring from the \(J=\mathbf{o}\) state to sublevels of the split \(J\,=\,1\) rotational state for initial \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) molecules adsorbed at the metal centers.4c,6b,6c,24 This assignment has been  confirmed  through  correlation  with  neutron diffraction and DFT calculations.29 Presumably, there is also a higher-energy transition, not collected within this current data range, as seen in the \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) series. The splitting between the low energy peaks of approximately 1.6 meV for both \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(m\mathrm{-dobdc})\) and \(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(m\mathrm{-dobdc})\) is smaller than that observed for any of the \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) materials. The position of the first peak is at higher energies than that of the first peak of the \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) frameworks  (except  for  the  Zn  analogue),  and  the position of the second peak is at lower energies than is observed for all the \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) compounds. The energy splitting of these peaks has been previously shown to have no correlation with the binding strength of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) at the open metal site.30
At loadings above 0.75 \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) molecules per metal, as sites II-IV begin to populate, the additional \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) molecules affect the rotational potential for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) molecules at site I. This adjusts the rotational energy level, resulting in a shift of the first peak to lower energies and the second peak to higher energies. These peak shifts are similar to those observed for the \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) frameworks, with magnitudes of approximately 0.7 meV for \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(m\mathrm{-dobdc})\) and 0.5 meV for \(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(m\mathrm{-dobdc})\), though much less than the 1.4 meV shift observed for \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\). Adsorption of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) at the secondary sites also results in a significant increase in the area of the peak at 9 meV and appearance of features near 12 and 15 meV as a new subset of rotational levels associated with the rotationally hindered second adsorption site. Transitions in this energy range are also observed in spectra reported for higher loadings of the \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) materials, indicating the similarity in adsorption potentials at these secondary sites.4c,6c
Infrared Spectra. Since the higher binding enthalpies and low-coverage \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) uptake cannot reasonably be attributed to a significant change in the macrostructure of the framework, infrared spectroscopy was used to further probe the binding of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) at the open metal coordination sites. The vibrational frequency of adsorbed \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) is almost always lower than that of the molecule in the gas phase (4161 cm\(^{-1}\)), and it is now well established that for metal-organic frameworks, there is a strong correlation between the magnitude of the frequency shift and the binding energy at a particular site.31 A comparison of the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) vibrational frequencies is shown at two different \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) loadings for \(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(m\mathrm{-dobdc})\), \(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\), \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(m\mathrm{-dobdc})\), and \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) (Figure 6) and for \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}(m\mathrm{-dobdc})\) and \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) (Figure S45). At this temperature, the pure vibrational part of the spectrum consists of an ortho-para pair that is separated by just 6 cm\(^{-1}\) in the gas phase. The
peak near \(4025~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) in each spectrum corresponds to the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) bound to the open metal site.
In each case, the spectra for loadings of \(0.5\ \mathrm{H}_{2}\) molecules per metal, which are known to arise purely from \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) bound to the open metal site, show a greater frequency shift for the \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(m\text{-dobdc})\) and \(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(m\text{-dobdc})\) than for \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\text{dobdc})\) or \(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(\text{dobdc})\). This shift is consistent with the stronger \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) binding observed in the \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(m\text{-dobdc})\) frameworks based on equilibrium adsorption isotherms and neutron diffraction studies. Upon further loading, the secondary adsorption sites begin to be populated, which is evidenced by a second peak that grows in around \(4125~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\). The energy of the peaks associated with the secondary sites is very similar to those seen for \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(\text{dobdc})\), consistent with the INS transitions for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) bound to the secondary sites, which were also similar between \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(m\text{-dobdc})\) and \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(\text{dobdc})\) compounds. This supports our hypothesis that the electronic structure at the metal center is significantly altered with the \(m\text{-dobdc}^{4-}\) linker, leading to a higher initial isosteric heat of adsorption, while the secondary sites in \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(m\text{-dobdc})\) and \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(\text{dobdc})\) are similar in binding potential due to structural similarities between the two frameworks. Furthermore, a consistent shift in the frequency for the metal-adsorbed \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) molecules as a function of concentration is seen for both the \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(m\text{-dobdc})\) and \(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(m\text{-dobdc})\) samples, although not to the same degree in the \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(m\text{-dobdc})\) sample. These concentration shifts have previously been attributed to \(\mathrm{H}_{2} \cdots \mathrm{H}_{2}\) interactions, and it is interesting to note that the shifts seem largely unaffected by the change in linker. Furthermore, these shifts correlate well with the shifts seen in rotational potential as the secondary binding sites are populated in the INS data shown in Figure 5.
Variable-temperature infrared spectroscopy is a standard technique for establishing the enthalpy of adsorption at a particular site.32 Figure 7 shows the
spectra obtained for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) in \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(m\text{-dobdc})\) while lowering the sample temperature from \(142\) to \(75~\mathrm{K}\). In each case, the initial \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) pressure for the system was set such that only the open metal site was occupied over the full temperature range. The fractional occupancy is then determined by the ratio of the area under the infrared band to that observed at complete saturation. The inset in Figure 7 shows the van’t Hoff relationship plot used to extract both the enthalpy and entropy change upon adsorption. The data obtained in this way for the different materials are summarized in Table 3. The slopes extracted from the van’t Hoff plots are sensitive to small variations in the maximum saturation area; as such, an error on the order of \(0.5~\mathrm{kJ/mol}\) is estimated. Even with these limitations, the data is consistent with the calculated isosteric heats of adsorption from the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) adsorption isotherms, showing an enthalpy increase from Mn to Co to Ni. More importantly, these infrared spectroscopy-based heat of adsorption values are about \(1.4~\mathrm{kJ/mol}\) larger than those calculated for their respective \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(\text{dobdc})\) counterparts from similar infrared experiments. The binding enthalpy of \(-13.7~\mathrm{kJ/mol}\) in \(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(m\text{-dobdc})\) is among the highest reported \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) binding enthalpies in a metal-organic framework to date. This is consistent with the trends observed using values calculated from \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) adsorption isotherms. We further note that the measured large entropy changes are consistent with previous studies, in which a strong correlation between the enthalpy and entropy change of the bound hydrogen in different metal-organic frameworks was observed.33 Overall, the redshift seen in the infrared spectra for the adsorbed \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) in \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(m\text{-dobdc})\) as compared to \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(\text{dobdc})\) indicates that the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) is more strongly bound and the variable-temperature data confirms the increased binding enthalpies seen from the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) adsorption data.
Electronic Structure Calculations. Density functional theory (DFT) was used to examine the differences in the electronic structures at the open metal sites by using representative complexes to model the \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(\text{dobdc})\) and \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(m\text{-dobdc})\) frameworks. DFT has shown itself to be a fairly robust method for modeling chemical systems; its major failings, self-interaction error and lack of dispersion interactions,34 are well-known and can be corrected for by using appropriate functionals that account for these failings, namely range-separation and explicit dispersion correction.35 Previously, the \(\omega\mathrm{B}97\mathrm{X}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{D}^{36}\)
functional was shown to accurately model \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) binding in metal-organic frameworks.37
Modeling \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) -type frameworks accurately with electronic  structure  theory  is  challenging  because fragmenting the structure at any point will lead to the neglect of important interactions from the chains formed by the \(\mathbf{M}^{2+}\) ions. In an attempt to understand the nature of the differences in hydrogen binding between \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) and \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(m\mathrm{-dobdc})\) ,  some  accuracy  in  the  calculated energetics  is  sacrificed  in  order  to  learn  relevant information  about  the  differences  between  the  two systems. In this vein, our modeling focused on the linker of interest coordinated to two \({\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}\) centers; the remaining ligands on each \({\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}\) ion were truncated as formaldehyde molecules in order to maintain charge balance. Since the key open coordination site for each \({\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}\) center in the framework is enforced by constraints on the ligands imposed by the macrostructure of the framework, which could  not  be  explicitly  included  in  our  model,  an unconstrained optimized geometry of the model would not  actually  reflect  the  structural  properties  of  the framework in question. Thus, the geometry of the linker complex was taken from the experimentally determined crystal structure of the framework and subsequently frozen before the interaction with \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) was optimized. Furthermore, the \(\mathrm{Co}{\cdots}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) distance was taken from the neutron diffraction spectra and used as a constraint to ensure an accurate depiction of this interaction in the model system.
While comparing absolute energy differences gives a single  number  to  describe \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) binding,  energy decomposition analysis (EDA) breaks down that number into physically interpretable components. An EDA based on  absolutely  localized  molecular  orbitals \((\mathrm{ALMOs})^{21}\) breaks down total binding energies into frozen energy, polarization, and charge transfer components. The frozen term  is  due  to  permanent  electrostatics  and  Pauli repulsions, since the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) electron density is being brought within the van der Waals radius of the Co atom. The polarization term corresponds to the favorable interaction of electrons in the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and complex fragments relaxing in the presence of the other fragment without electron
transfer. Charge transfer stems from energy lowering when electrons are allowed to flow from one fragment to the other. A recent generalization of the ALMO EDA to open-shell molecules allows for the application of this method to general metal-containing systems.38
The binding energy decomposition analysis clarifies the differences in these two species (Table 4). Specifically, there is more charge transfer and polarization in the \(m\cdot\) - dobdc4- containing complex, which is accompanied by a partially  offsetting  increase  in  the  energetically unfavorable frozen term. This larger frozen term is the result of the increased steric repulsion that stems from a shorter \(\mathrm{Co}{\cdots}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) distance in the \(m\) -dobdc4- complex. The increased charge transfer energy of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) binding in this species is key, as that is the largest term in the total calculated binding energy. A breakdown of the charge transfer term can be seen in Table 5; in both cases, the largest portion of the charge transfer comes from the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\,\upsigma\) orbital to the unoccupied orbitals of the Co complex. The increased \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) to Co forward donation is indicative of more positive charge at the metal cation in the \(m\) -dobdc4- complex as compared with the dobdc4- complex. This explains  the  experimentally  determined  increase  in binding enthalpy of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) in the \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(m\mathrm{-dobdc})\) frameworks as compared with their \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) analogues, as increased positive charge at the metal center leads to stronger polarization of the bound \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) . Additionally, a difference is seen in the location of the bound \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) relative to the linker in the two complexes. In the dobdc4- linker complex, the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) binds to create a nearly octahedral geometry around the \({\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}\) ion, with a distance of \(3\,\mathrm{ps}\,\mathrm{\AA}\) from the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) to the aromatic carbon bonded to the carboxylate group (the
alpha carbon). Conversely, in the \( m \)-dobdc\(^{4-}\) complex, the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) is reoriented toward the linker at a distance of \( 2.64 \, \text{\AA} \) from the alpha carbon (Figure 8a-b). This difference suggests that a change in the charge at the alpha carbon due to the different symmetry of the \( m \)-dobdc\(^{4-}\) linker possibly facilitates a change in the binding geometry for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\). While this significant change may be an artifact associated with the small cluster models, it is the same trend seen experimentally for the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\)···alpha carbon distance, which is \( 3.48(3) \, \text{\AA} \) in \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(m \)-dobdc) and \( 3.59(2) \, \text{\AA} \) in \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\text{dobdc})\).
The orbital interactions between \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and the \(\mathrm{Co}\) complexes provide further insight into the stronger \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) binding in \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(m \)-dobdc). While increased forward bonding is a product of increased charge density at the metal, the nature of the backbonding from the complex to \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) is quite different. This can be seen by looking at the complementary occupied virtual orbital pairs (COVPs) that contribute most to backbonding (Figure 8c-d). In the dobdc\(^{4-}\) complex, the occupied orbital is primarily localized on the metal center, while in the \( m \)-dobdc\(^{4-}\) species, the donating orbital includes contributions from the \(\pi\) system of the linker. This increased backbonding for \( m \)-dobdc\(^{4-}\) agrees with the redshift seen in the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) infrared spectra for the \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(m \)-dobdc) frameworks, as increased backbonding will weaken the \(\mathrm{H}-\mathrm{H}\) bond. This possible extra interaction with the linker in the \( m \)-dobdc\(^{4-}\) complex, coupled with the larger forward donation of the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) to the more positively charged metal center, are the key differences between these two systems that help to explain the stronger \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) binding seen in \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(m \)-dobdc) versus \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(\text{dobdc})\).
## Conclusions
The foregoing results demonstrate the synthesis of a new family of metal-organic frameworks, \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(m \)-dobdc) \((\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}, \mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}, \mathbf{Fe}, \mathbf{Co}, \mathbf{Ni})\), representing a potentially less expensive version of their well-known structural isomers \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(\text{dobdc})\). Hydrogen was used as an initial probe to determine how electronic and structural differences in the new frameworks modified the interactions of the open metal coordination sites with adsorbates. The Mn, Fe, Co, and Ni variants exhibited greater isosteric heats of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) adsorption by approximately \( 1 \, \text{kJ/mol} \) compared to the \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(\text{dobdc})\) compounds. The results from powder neutron diffraction, inelastic neutron scattering, and infrared spectroscopy experiments performed on hydrogen-loaded samples of \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(m \)-dobdc) all support this as arising from stronger interactions between the metal centers and the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) molecules. Future efforts will focus on the larger-scale production of \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(m \)-dobdc) compounds, and determining whether the enhanced charge density at the metal sites leads to improvements compared to their \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(\text{dobdc})\) analogues in the efficacy of key gas separations, including \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) from air,\(^{ij,39}\) \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2},^{1\mathrm{h},\mathrm{j},7\mathrm{a},40}\) \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2},^{1\mathrm{j},41}\) \(\mathrm{CO}/\mathrm{H}_{2},^{1\mathrm{e},39\mathrm{a},42}\) ethylene/ethane,\(^{ii,9}\) propylene/propane,\(^{ii,3\mathrm{d},9}\) and methane purification.\(^{43}\)
